--TEST--
`$a[]` is refused everywhere but as an assignment target, PHP's "Cannot use [] for reading" taken at compile time — `rule:types/absent-storage-is-never-a-zero-value`, `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `[]` names the key one past the highest integer key, which is an answer only
// where a value is being put there. PHP refuses `echo $a[];` and `unset($a[])`
// at compile time for exactly that reason. `$a[] .= "x"` is the one spelling
// PHP accepts — it appends, because the element that is not there yet reads as
// `""` — and `rule:types/absent-storage-is-never-a-zero-value` declines that coercion, so Novis refuses it
// alongside the other two rather than inventing a zero value for an absent
// element. An append written *inside* a subscript is a read of the inner one
// and not a second target, so the walk that legitimizes a target chain never
// reaches it.
array<string> $a = [];
$a[] = "kept";
echo $a[], "\n";
unset($a[]);
$a[] .= "x";
$a[$a[]] = "y";
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0481]: `[]` can only be assigned to
   --> case.nvs:13:6
%A
error[E0481]: `[]` can only be assigned to
   --> case.nvs:14:7
%A
error[E0481]: `[]` can only be assigned to
